Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 15:23:41 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 12/71] Refresh the project's AGENTS.md from the conversation
 before compacting

A compaction summary dies with the session, but users of a subscription
CLI resume the next day. AGENTS.md is already loaded as user instructions
at the start of every session, so the file the agent keeps current is what
actually carries today's conversation into tomorrow's. Compaction is the
moment that detail is about to be discarded, which makes it the moment to
persist what is worth keeping.

This is not a special mechanism: it is one ordinary agent turn with the
ordinary toolset, asked to update a file, exactly as if the user had typed
"update AGENTS.md". No bespoke tool, no custom patch parser, no token cap,
no iteration limit. The prompt is framed as a system procedure the way the
compaction prompt is, and it tells the agent to restructure the file freely
rather than chase a minimal diff.

The turn is invisible. Its items are recorded so the agent sees its own
reads and edits while it runs, but they carry the turn's id as their
invisibility owner and are dropped from every later prompt, so the
compaction that follows sees history byte-identical to what it would have
seen had this never run. `run_sampling_request` is called directly rather
than `run_turn`, whose first act is a pre-sampling compaction check that
would re-enter the compaction already in progress.

Failure never reaches the caller: compaction proceeds either way. The model
call is not retried here because `run_sampling_request` already retries it
and filters the errors retrying cannot fix; only `capture_step_context` is,
since it is local and costs no tokens.

Behind `project_memory`, on by default, and skipped for untrusted projects.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
